{{!-- The library base the panels resolve catalogue requests against. In
      the head rather than at the foot, where it used to sit: the
      partials now bring their own scripts and those render inside
      <main>, so a meta tag after them is a meta tag below its readers.
      It happens to work either way, because the only panel script that
      reads it (lib/faith-dictionary-refs.js) reads it lazily inside
      base() and falls back to this same URL. Depending on that is how
      the fallback quietly becomes the thing actually serving. --}}
<meta name="tfr-library-base" content="https://mo-tfr-library.mo-podcast-feed.workers.dev" />
{{/contentFor}}
{{!-- /dashboard/faith-received/ — what the reader has kept from the
     library: the WORKS they saved, and the PASSAGES they kept.

     THIS PAGE USED TO BE ITS OWN IMPLEMENTATION, AND THAT WAS THE BUG.
     It rendered a bare <ol> out of assets/js/faith-saved.js over eleven
     lines of CSS (`columns: 2` and a hairline), which is what Ian saw
     and called a mess. Worse than the look: it showed bookmarks ONLY.
     Every passage the reader had kept — the whole notebook, which is
     the thing they actually did work to make — was not on the page at
     all, on a page whose own heading said "saved from the library".

     So this template no longer renders a list. The Research page's
     Bookmarks and Notebook workspaces are the finished, reviewed
     presentation of exactly these two stores, and both partials are
     written to be dropped into a shell owned by someone else: they
     bring their own <script> tags, they bind once and return on a
     second run, and a host that also loads one of their scripts is
     harmless. See the headers of _bookmarks-panel.hbs and
     _notebook-panel.hbs.

     THE BAND CARRIES .faith-research-band, AND THAT IS NOT A STRAY
     CLASS. Those partials' headers say to wrap them in a plain
     `<section class="bband">` because "nothing in here depends on the
     tab shell". Their SCRIPTS do not. Their PRESENTATION does: roughly
     twenty selector lists in assets/css/faith-received.css (from
     ~:19826, "RESEARCH HUB: THIN-LINE BOXES, THE WHOLE WIDTH", Ian,
     2026-09-23) are scoped to .faith-research-band, and they are what
     turn hairline rows into the card grid, release the 720px column to
     the full workspace, put the controls in a cream panel, and — this
     one matters — cancel the panel's own full-bleed breakout so it does
     not draw a white band inside the workspace's white band. Without
     the class this page renders the base form, which is "everything was
     hairline rows on white, with nothing boxed": bullet three of the
     list of defects that block was written to fix. The class is how the
     two surfaces are the same surface.

     WHAT THE DASHBOARD HAS TO GIVE BACK. .bband.faith-research-band is
     tuned for the Research page, where a tab strip sits above the
     workspace and one panel shows at a time, so it zeroes its own top
     padding and border. Here two bands sit one under the other with no
     strip, and each panel already brings a white ground between two
     hairlines plus 48/44 of inner padding. So the band contributes
     nothing at all and the doubled hairline at the seam collapses —
     see .dashboard .bband.faith-research-band in faith-received.css.
     Stacking the band's padding on top of the panel's own is the
     "screenful of cream" that rule's own comment is about.

     TWO BANDS, NOT TABS. The Research page gives these panels a tab
     strip because it has seven workspaces and room for one at a time.
     Here there are two and they answer different questions ("where am I
     in Augustine" is not "what have I gathered about grace"), so both
     are on the page and the reader scrolls. A tab strip over two panels
     hides half the page to save nothing.

     THE STORAGE LINE IN THE HERO IS LOAD-BEARING. Each panel says where
     its own things live, but only once it has something to say it
     about: the bookmarks note is hidden until a row has a place to
     resume to, and the notebook's until there is a passage. So on one
     page the two notes rarely appear together, and a reader with
     passages and no saved works would read "kept in this browser only"
     as a fact about the whole page. It is not, and believing it is how
     someone loses work. Said once, in the hero, unconditionally.

     WHY THE GATE IS @member AND NOT paid/comped. The sibling dashboard
     pages (custom-dashboard-bookmarks.hbs and the rest) gate on paid or
     comped. This one must not: saving a work from the library follows
     the TFR beta tier, which is any signed-in member
     (assets/js/lib/faith-work-bookmarks.js, `beta ? signedIn : paid`).
     Gating this page harder than the Save button that fills it would
     let a free member save a work during the beta and then be told the
     page listing it is a member feature. If the beta ever closes, that
     file's constant is what changes and this gate follows it.

     The Bookmarks panel used to do exactly that — hardcode paid/comped
     while the Save button honoured the beta — so building this page on
     it meant fixing it: assets/js/page/faith-bookmarks.js now reads the
     same constant, which also corrects the Research page's Bookmarks
     tab. mo-kit never had the bug; it deliberately serves the read to
     any signed-in member so someone whose card lapses can still see and
     clear what they saved. The Notebook has never been tier-gated; it
     is localStorage and there is no tier to check. --}}

<main class="dashboard">

  {{#unless @member}}
    <section class="hero dashboard-hero">
      <div class="container">
        <p class="hero-kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Member Dashboard</p>
        <h1 class="hero-headline">Sign in to see what you have <span class="highlight"><em>kept</em></span>.</h1>
        <p class="hero-sub">The works you have saved from the library and the passages you have kept.</p>
        <p class="dashboard-actions">
          <a href="#/portal/signin" class="btn btn-solid btn-primary" data-portal="signin">Sign in</a>
          <a href="/membership/" class="btn btn-ghost">Become a Member</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  {{else}}
    <section class="hero dashboard-hero">
      <div class="container">
        <p class="hero-kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Member Dashboard</p>
        <h1 class="hero-headline">The <span class="highlight"><em>Faith Received</em></span></h1>
        <p class="hero-sub">The works you have saved and the passages you have kept.</p>
        <p class="hero-sub dashboard-faith-note">Saved works are kept with your membership and follow you between devices. Kept passages, and the places inside a work, are stored in this browser only.</p>
        <p class="dashboard-actions">
          <a href="/dashboard/" class="btn btn-ghost">Back to dashboard</a>
          <a href="/the-faith-received/curated/" class="btn btn-solid btn-primary">Browse the library</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    {{!-- No aria-label on either band: each panel opens with its own
          <h2> ("Bookmarks", "Notebook"), and a region named "Saved
          works" wrapped around a heading that says "Bookmarks" is two
          names for one thing in a screen reader. --}}
    <section class="bband faith-research-band">
      <div class="research-workspace">
        <div class="research-workspace-inner">
          {{> "faith-received/_bookmarks-panel"}}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {{!-- Kept passages, and the collections they are gathered into.
          Collections are a filter inside this panel, not a third band:
          a collection has only ever held entry ids into this same
          notebook. The reasoning is in _notebook-panel.hbs. --}}
    <section class="bband faith-research-band">
      <div class="research-workspace">
        <div class="research-workspace-inner">
          {{> "faith-received/_notebook-panel"}}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  {{/unless}}

</main>
